<p>The iron maiden, the medieval torture device consisting of a hinged cabinet with a spike-lined interior, seems to have been a myth. There&#8217;s no mention of it before the 19th century. </p>
<p>Bielefeld University legal historian Wolfgang Schild suggests that enterprising exhibitors may have pieced together the first specimens using artifacts gathered from museum collections, to attract paying customers. It&#8217;s possible that German philosopher Johann Philipp Siebenkees invented a history for the device &#8212; in his writings he claims that it was first used on Aug. 14, 1515, to execute a coin forger. But there&#8217;s no reliable evidence for it in the Middle Ages.</p>
